WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.917
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Building
your infrastructure

00:00:01.917 --> 00:00:04.320
or hosting your application
in the Cloud can be tricky.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.360
One misconfiguration
and your costs

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.640
could quietly creep
up or even skyrocket.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.300
Your performance
could slow to a crawl,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.600
or you could have holes in
your security big enough

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.700
to cause some serious trouble.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.710
It can be difficult to
stay on top of things,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.510
but you don't have
to do it alone.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.910
Today, I'm going to tell
you about Active Assist,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.890
the intelligence baked
into Google Cloud that

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.560
helps you troubleshoot,
configure, and optimize

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.240
your infrastructure with ease.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.610
So what is Active Assist?

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.540
Well, it's not just one thing.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.690
It's a portfolio of
tools and services

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.310
that span across Google Cloud.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.580
Active Assist combines data,
intelligence, and machine

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.310
learning to provide you with
an array of tools specifically

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:42.593
tuned to your usage.

00:00:42.593 --> 00:00:44.010
These tools are
designed to reduce

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.070
the complexity of managing
infrastructure in the Cloud.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.350
The Active Assist
portfolio includes

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.720
the recommendation
hub, and an API,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.820
as well as a collection
of Recommenders.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.650
Let's break down
what Recommenders are

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.120
and how they work.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.060
Recommenders offer personalized
intelligent insights

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.940
and recommendations for
various Google Cloud resources

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.640
and services based
on your usage data.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.710
These recommendations
expose opportunities

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.430
to fine-tune and optimize your
configuration in various areas.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.580
For example, if you run virtual
machines on Compute Engine,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.950
you can reduce costs by
shutting down or deleting

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.010
any virtual machines that
aren't actively being used.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.250
The Idle VM Recommender will
let when a virtual machine

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.870
is idle or unused.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.110
Recommendations are made on
based on Cloud monitoring data

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.610
and are continuously
being updated.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.790
Another example is
enforcing the best practice

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.380
of least privilege.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.390
This includes making
sure that users only

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.300
have the permissions that
they actually need and use.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.600
The IAM Recommender will spot
any excess permissions granted

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.000
to any accounts and
offer alternative roles

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.640
and permissions
to grant instead.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.710
To view or manage
recommendations,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.230
users will require the
appropriate IAM roles

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.970
corresponding to the
type of Recommender

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.440
that they need access to.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.810
These permissions are also
bundled in some roles,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.160
like the Owner role.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.860
That way, you can delegate
the visibility and management

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.530
of recommendations to
different users or teams.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.510
Once you have access
to recommendations,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.330
there's a number of ways that
you can view and manage them.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.760
First, you might
see them in context

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.820
on the pages for the services
that they correspond to.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.970
For example, you'll see
IAM policy recommendations

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.110
at the top of the
IAM and Admin page

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.370
and next to any accounts that
might have excess permissions.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.623
In the same way, the Compute
Engine VM Instances page

00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:29.040
will tell you when there are
machine type recommendations,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.590
at the top of the page
and next to any machines

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.540
that the recommendations
apply to.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.580
This visibility ensures
that administrators

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.230
for these services
don't miss any

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.450
of the latest recommendations
that could positively

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.400
impact the organization.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.980
Recommenders can also be
accessed from the Cloud Console

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.400
via the recommendation hub,
where you can view, dismiss,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.310
or apply recommendations
with just a few clicks.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.290
In the recommendation
hub, each Recommender

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.570
will display with a card
with its description

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.130
and a summary of
the recommendations

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.220
that it's generated, along with
the impact of actually applying

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.300
those changes.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.520
You can click View All
for any Recommender

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.054
to see more details about the
individual recommendation.

00:03:06.054 --> 00:03:07.560
Recommendations can
also be accessed

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.480
from the gcloud
command line interface

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.390
for more advanced and
programmatic management.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.400
Accessing recommendations
through the CLI

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.590
reveals additional
information and properties,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.800
such as priority and
primary impact category.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.740
These properties are helpful
in identifying recommendations

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.440
that could have the biggest
effect on costs, performance,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.120
and security.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.625
By familiarizing
yourself with some

00:03:28.625 --> 00:03:30.000
of the features
of Active Assist,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.460
you can reduce the complexity
of running the cloud.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.947
A great place to
start is getting

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:36.030
to know all the different
Recommenders that Active

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.440
Assist has to offer.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.390
Collectively, Recommenders
will equip you

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.760
to do six things successfully--

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.640
manage your costs wisely,
maximize the performance

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.270
of your systems, mitigate your
security risk proactively,

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.180
deliver high availability and
reliable services to your end

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.780
users, spend less time managing
your cloud configuration,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.320
and reducing the carbon
footprint of your workloads.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.570
In the next few videos, we're
going to focus primarily

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:01.680
on three areas--

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.530
costs, performance,
and security.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.170
Then we'll take a look at how
we can manage recommendations

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.420
across multiple projects inside
of a single organization.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:11.920
Stay tuned for the
next video, where

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.837
we start by taking a
look at what Recommenders

00:04:13.837 --> 00:04:15.540
can do for cost optimization.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.420
See you there.

